nt in medicine: The analysis of method comparison studies</article-title><source>Statistician</source><year>1983</year><volume>32</volume><fpage>307</fpage><lpage>317</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Houtkooper</surname><given-names>LB</given-names></name><name><surname>Lohman</surname><given-names>TG</given-names></name><name><surname>Going</surname><given-names>SB</given-names></name><name><surname>Hall</surname><given-names>MC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Validity of bioelectrical impedance for body composition assessment in children</article-title><source>J Appl Physiol</source><year>1989</year><volume>66</volume><fpage>814</fpage><lpage>821</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2708210</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lukaski</surname><given-names>HC</given-names></name><name><surname>Bolonchuk</surname><given-names>WW</given-names></name><name><surname>Siders</surname><given-names>WA</given-names></name><name><surname>Hall</surname><given-names>CB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Body composition assessement of athletes using bioelectrical impedance measurements</article-title><source>J Sports Med Phys Fitness</source><year>1990</year><volume>30</volume><fpage>434</fpage><lpage>440</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2079851</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Liang</surname><given-names>MTC</given-names></name><name><surname>Norris</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effects of skin blood flow and temperature on bioelectrical impedance after exercise</article-title><source>Med Sci Sports Exerc</source><year>1993</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>1231</fpage><lpage>1239</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8289609</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Goss</surname><given-names>FL</given-names></name><name><surname>Robertson</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Dube</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Rutkowski</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Andreacci</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Lenz</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Ranalli</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Frazee</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Does exercise testing affect the bioelectrical impedance assessment of body composition in children?</article-title><source>Ped Ex Sci</source><year>2003</year><volume>15</volume><fpage>216</fpage><lpage>222</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>